    "task_type": "task1",
    "task_prompt": "The pie charts show figures for the use of public libraries in 1991 and 2000 in Britain. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.",
    "sample_answer": "The pie charts compare the purposes of visits to public libraries in Britain in 1991 and 2000, while also providing the total number of library visitors in each year.\nOverall, the total number of library users increased over the period, whereas borrowing and returning books remained the principal reason for visiting despite becoming less prevalent. By contrast, reading newspapers or magazines and studying both accounted for smaller proportions in 2000, while obtaining information and borrowing or returning videos gained popularity.\nIn 1991, public libraries attracted a total of 290 million visitors. Borrowing and returning books was by far the most common activity, accounting for 65% of all visits. Reading newspapers and magazines ranked second at 15%, while obtaining information and studying were equally popular, each representing 10% of library use. Borrowing or returning videos was not recorded in this year.\nBy 2000, annual library attendance had risen to 350 million. Although the proportion of visitors borrowing or returning books fell to 55%, it remained the dominant purpose. The share of those seeking information doubled from 10% to 20%, overtaking newspaper and magazine reading, which declined sharply to just 5%. Borrowing or returning videos emerged as a new category at 18%, whereas studying experienced the steepest fall, dropping to only 2%.\n(210 words)",
    "tips": [],
    "difficulty": "medium",
    "status": "published",
    "image_path": "writing_data/pie_chart_public_library.png"
